          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a document refers to the J45 family, is that a code or a category?",
          "answer": "**J45 is a category, not a single complete code.** The asthma document says the category \"is never used at three characters.\" Its remaining characters come from two independent axes that are crossed with each other, so the specific codes sit beneath J45 (source: *Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*).\n\n- **Severity axis:** mild intermittent, mild persistent, moderate persistent, severe persistent, and an \"other and unspecified\" option for asthma documented without a severity level.\n- **Exacerbation axis:** uncomplicated, with acute exacerbation, or with status asthmaticus.\n- **Named variants:** exercise-induced bronchospasm and cough variant asthma have their own codes. Occupational asthma linked to a known agent is classified elsewhere, under lung diseases due to external agents.\n\nThe corpus does not list the exact subcode strings, so I can't give them here. A coder or the ICD-10-CM tabular list would show them.\n\n**What \"family\" means:** My search for a definition failed, and the passages I retrieved do not define the word \"family\" in a coding document. I can't confirm its meaning from this source. In everyday use, people often say \"family\" to mean a category and all its subcodes together, but please check the specific document or a coding reference for its exact meaning.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
